116: \title{Presenting the cohomology of a Schubert variety:\\ Proof of the minimality conjecture}
117: \author{Avery St.~Dizier}
118: \address{Dept.~of Mathematics, Michigan State University, East Lansing, MI 48824, USA}
119: \email{stdizier@msu.edu}
120: \author{Alexander Yong}
121: \address{Dept.~of Mathematics, U.~Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, Urbana, IL 61801, USA}
122: \email{ayong@illinois.edu}
123: \date{August 31, 2023}
124: \begin{abstract}
125: A minimal presentation of the cohomology ring of the flag manifold $GL_n/B$ was given in [A.~Borel, 1953]. This presentation was extended
126: by [E.~Akyildiz--A.~Lascoux--P.~Pragacz, 1992] to a non-minimal one for all Schubert varieties. Work of [V.~Gasharov--V.~Reiner, 2002] gave a short, \emph{i.e.} polynomial-size,
127: presentation for a subclass of Schubert varieties that includes the smooth ones. In [V.~Reiner--A.~Woo--A.~Yong, 2011], a general shortening was found; it implies an exponential upper bound of $2^n$ on the number of generators required. That work states
128: a minimality
129: conjecture whose significance would be an exponential lower bound of $\frac{\sqrt{2}^{n+2}}{\sqrt{\pi n}}$ on the number
130: of generators needed in worst case, giving the first obstructions to short presentations. We prove the minimality conjecture. Our proof uses the Hopf algebra structure of the ring of symmetric functions.
131: \end{abstract}
132: \maketitle
133: \section{Introduction}
134: \subsection{Background} Let $X=Fl_n({\mathbb C})$ be the \emph{complete flag manifold}; its points are complete flags of subspaces of ${\mathbb C}^n$,
135: \[F_{\bullet}=\{\langle \vec 0\rangle\subset F_1\subset F_2\subset \cdots\subset F_{n-1}\subset {\mathbb C}^n\},\]
136: where $F_i$ is a $i$-dimensional linear subspace of ${\mathbb C}^n$.
137: In 1953, A.~Borel \cite{Borel} gave a presentation of its integral cohomology ring:
138: \[H^{*}(X)\cong {\mathbb{Z}}[x_1,\ldots,x_n]/I^{S_n},\]
139: where $I^{S_n}$ is the ideal generated by symmetric polynomials of positive degree. A feature of Borel's presentation is its \emph{shortness}: the ideal $I^{S_n}$ is generated by the elementary symmetric polynomials
140: $e_{i}(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$, $1\leq i\leq n$. This exhibits that $H^*(X)$ is a complete intersection, with $n$ generators and $n$ relations.
141: Let $GL_n$ be the Lie group of invertible $n\times n$ matrices and $B$ its Borel subgroup of invertible upper triangular matrices. $GL_n$ acts transitively on $X$
142: whereas $B$ is the stabilizer of the standard basis flag given by $F_i:={\rm Span} \ \{\vec e_1, \vec e_2,\ldots, \vec e_i\}$. By the orbit-stabilizer theorem, $X$ may be
143: identified (topologically) with $GL_n/B$. The finitely many $B$-orbits of $X$ are called the \emph{Schubert cells} $X_w^{\circ}$ and are indexed by permutations $w$ in the symmetric group $S_n$ of permutations of $\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$. Each cell is isomorphic to the affine space
144: ${\mathbb C}^{\ell(w)}$. Together, they form a CW-decomposition for $X$ where each cell has even real dimension.
145: Their closures, the \emph{Schubert varieties}
146: \[X_w\coloneqq \overline{X_w^{\circ}},\]
147: provide a ${\mathbb Z}$-basis of the integral homology $H_*(X)$ and their
148: Poincar\'e duals
149: $\sigma_w=[X_w]^*$
150: give a ${\mathbb Z}$-basis for the integral cohomology ring $H^*(X)$.
151: The \emph{Bruhat decomposition} is
152: \[X_w=\coprod_{u\leq w} X_u^{\circ},\]
153: where $u\leq w$ refers to \emph{(strong) Bruhat order} on $S_n$. Thus, $X_w$ inherits a CW-decomposition from $Fl_n$. The map on cohomology
154: \[H^*(X)\to H^*(X_w)\]
155: that is induced by the inclusion of $X_w$ in $Fl_n$ is a surjection with kernel
156: \begin{equation}
157: \label{eqn:apriorigens}
158: I_w\coloneqq \mathrm{Span}_\mathbb{Z} \left\{\sigma_u\mid u\not\leq w\right\};
159: \end{equation}
160: see \cite{RWY}. Therefore one obtains a Borel-type presentation
161: \[H^*(X_w)\cong H^*(X)/I_w
162: .\]
163: The list of generators for $I_w$ given in (\ref{eqn:apriorigens}) is quite redundant in general since they span $I_w$ \emph{linearly}. What are more efficient lists of generators for $I_w$?
164: In 1992, E.~Akyildiz--A.~Lascoux--P.~Pragacz \cite{ALP} made the first step towards minimizing the generators of $I_w$. To state their result, define a \emph{grassmannian} permutation to be $u\in S_n$ with a unique descent,
165: \emph{i.e.}, a position $k$ such that $u(k)>u(k+1)$.
166: \begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem~2.2]{ALP}}]\label{thm:ALP}
167: For any $w\in S_n$, the ideal $I_w$ defining $H^*(X_w)$ as a quotient of $H^*(X)$ is generated
168: by the cohomology classes $\sigma_u$ where $u\not\leq w$ and $u$ is grassmannian.
169: \end{theorem}
170: In 2002, V.~Gasharov--V.~Reiner \cite{Gasharov.Reiner} showed that when $X_w$
171: is \emph{defined by inclusions} (a family of Schubert varieties that include the smooth ones) then $I_w$ can be generated by $n^2$ many generators. In addition, for the subclass of \emph{Ding's Schubert varieties} \cite{Ding1,Ding2}, they offered an even smaller generating set consisting of $n$ generators; an application is
172: given by M.~Develin--J.~Martin--V.~Reiner \cite{DMR}.
173: Can one always give such a ``short'' presentation of
174: $H^*(X_w)$? Formally, one asks:
175: \begin{center}
176: \emph{Is it always possible to generate $I_w$ by $O({\sf poly}(n))$ many generators?}
177: \end{center}
178: In 2011, V.~Reiner, A.~Woo, and the second author \cite{RWY} further refined Theorem~\ref{thm:ALP}. Complementing this result was a minimality conjecture which, if true, implies a
179: negative answer to the above question. That is, there is a family of Schubert varieties $X_{w^{(n)}}$ for which $I_{w^{(n)}}$ requires exponentially many generators.
180: \subsection{The main result}
181: The goal of this paper is to prove the minimality conjecture of \cite{RWY}.
182: A useful way to think about Bruhat order was introduced by A.~Lascoux and M.-P.~Schutzenberger \cite{LS}.
183: They show there is a ``base'' $B\subset S_n$ which is minimal with respect to set-theoretic inclusion such that the
184: map
185: \[\varphi: (S_n,\leq ) \to (2^B,\subseteq), \ u\mapsto \{b\in B| b\leq u\}\]
186: is a poset isomorphism of $(S_n,\leq )$ with its image. In fact, $B$ consists of the \emph{bigrassmannian} permutations, namely, those $v\in S_n$ such that both $v$ and $v^{-1}$ are grassmannian.
187: The bigrassmannian permutations (besides the identity) are indexed by integers $r,s,t$ such that
188: $1\leq t\leq r,s\leq n$ and $t>r+s-n$. Let $v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$ be the bigrassmannian permutation uniquely
189: characterized by having unique descent at $r$, $v^{-1}_{r,s,t,n}$ having descent at $s$ and $v_{r,s,t,n}(t)=s+1$.
190: In one-line notation, one explicitly has
191: \begin{multline}\nonumber
192: v_{r,s,t,n}=1,2,\ldots,t-1,\ s+1,s+2,\ldots,s+r-t+1,\\
193: t,t+1,\ldots,s, \
194: s+r-t+2,s+r-t+3,\ldots,n;
195: \end{multline}
196: see \cite[Lemma~4.1]{RWY}.
197: For each basic element of $S_n$, a bigrassmannian permutation
198: $v$, define the \emph{basic ideal} of $H^*(X)$ as
199: \[J_v\coloneqq \mathrm{Span}_\mathbb{Z}\left\{\sigma_u\mid u\geq v\right\}.\]
200: It is shown in \cite{RWY} that $I_w$ decomposes into basic ideals. Specifically,
201: \begin{equation}
202: \label{eqn:thedecompabc}
203: I_w=\sum_{v\in {\mathcal E}(w)} J_v,
204: \end{equation}
205: where ${\mathcal E}(w)$ is the set of $u \in S_n$ which are minimal in the Bruhat order among those not
206: below $w$. The set ${\mathcal E}(w)$ is referred to as the \emph{essential set}, and consists only of bigrassmannian permutations \cite[Theorem 1.1]{RWY}.
207: As explained in \cite[Section~3]{RWY}, the projection
208: \[X\to \mathrm{Gr}_{r,n}\]
209: onto the Grassmannian of $r$-planes in $\mathbb{C}^n$
210: induces a split-inclusion $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\xhookrightarrow{\iota} H^*(X)$. Consequently (see \cite[Proposition 3.1]{RWY}), elements of $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$ generate $J_v$ in $H^*(X)$ if and only if they generate the contraction $J_v\cap H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$ inside $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$.
211: In particular, elements of $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$ \emph{minimally} generate $J_v$ in $H^*(X)$ if and only if they \emph{minimally} generate $J_v\cap H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$ in $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$. This allows one to study the generation of $J_v$ inside $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$ instead of $H^*(X)$ without loss of generality, enabling the use of symmetric function theory.
212: %It is established in \cite[Section~3]{RWY} (see specifically Proposition~3.1 and Theorem~3.2) that, without loss, the ideals $J_v$ of $H^*(X)$ can be considered instead in the cohomology ring $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})$ of the Grassmannian of $r$-planes in $\mathbb{C}^n$. This allows one to study $J_v$ by working with symmetric functions.
213: Let $\Lambda=\Lambda(x_1,x_2,\ldots)$ be the ring of symmetric functions with integral coefficients. This space has a ${\mathbb Z}$-linear basis consisting of \emph{Schur functions} $s_{\lambda}$,
214: where $\lambda$ is an integer partition. Consider the ideal
215: \[I_{r,n-r} = \mathrm{Span}_\mathbb{Z}\left\{s_\lambda\mid \lambda\nsubseteq (n-r)^r \right\} \]
216: of $\Lambda$. Then one has the following standard presentation of the cohomology of Grassmannians:
217: \[H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\cong \Lambda/I_{r,n-r}. \]
218: We will use an overline to denote the projection $\Lambda\to\Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$. Specifically, we will write $\overline{\Lambda}$ as a shorthand for $\Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$. We will also distinguish between $s_{\lambda}\in \Lambda$, the usual Schur function, and $\overline{s}_{\lambda}$ for its image in $\overline{\Lambda}$.
219: We resolve the minimality conjecture \cite[Conjecture~4.10]{RWY} about the basic ideals $J_v$:
220: \begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
221: Given a bigrassmannian permutation $v=v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$ let
222: \begin{align*}
223: i&\coloneqq s-t+1,\\
224: j&\coloneqq r-t+1,\\
225: a&\coloneqq \min(n-r-i,r-j),\\
226: b&\coloneqq \min(i,j).
227: \end{align*}
228: Then the basic ideal $J_v$, thought of as an ideal of $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\cong \Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$, is minimally generated by
229: \begin{equation}
230: \label{eqn:minimallist}
231: \{\overline{s}_{\mu} \mid i^j\subseteq \mu \subseteq (i^j,b^a)\}.
232: \end{equation}
233: \end{theorem}
234: That (\ref{eqn:minimallist}) generates $J_v$ is \cite[Theorem 4.8]{RWY}. The conjectural part, which we prove,
235: is the minimality claim, that is, no generator can be removed without changing the ideal.
236: As explained in \cite[Section~4.4]{RWY}, the minimality from Theorem~\ref{thm:main}
237: implies obstructions to short presentations of $H^*(X_w)$. First, let
238: $w^{(n)}:=w_{r,s,t,n}$ be defined as in \cite[Corollary~4.5]{RWY}; explicitly, by \cite[Section~8]{Reading},
239: \begin{multline}
240: w_{r,s,t,n}=n,n-1,\ldots,(n-r+t+1), s,s-1,\ldots,s-t+1,\\
241: n-r+t,n-r+t-3,\ldots,s+1,s-t,s-t-1,\ldots,1.
242: \end{multline}
243: Then
244: \begin{equation}
245: \label{eqn:I=J}
246: I_{w^{(n)}}=J_{v_{r,s,t,n}}.
247: \end{equation}
248: Second, let $n=4m$, $r=s=2m$, and $t=m+1$ for some positive integer $m$. Thus $r=n-r=2m$, $i=j=m$, and $a=b=m$.
249: Therefore, Theorem~\ref{thm:main} combined with (\ref{eqn:I=J}) implies $I_{w^{(n)}}$ requires an exponentially growing number
250: \[{2m\choose m}\sim \frac{4^{m}}{\sqrt{\pi m}}=\frac{(\sqrt{2})^{n+2}}{\sqrt{\pi n}}\]
251: of generators. This proves the first exponential lower bound to accompany the $2^n$ upper bound
252: established in \cite{RWY}.
253: \subsection{Organization} The remainder of this paper contains our proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
254: Our proof strategy is as follows. If the generators (\ref{eqn:minimallist}) are not minimal, there is a
255: syzygy of the form (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}). In Section~\ref{sec:theequations}, using the famous Littlewood--Richardson rule, this can be rephrased as a nontrivial solution to a system of linear equations. We divide the system into two subfamilies, ``tall'' and ``wide'' equations (Section~\ref{sec:theequations}). All of these
256: equations are homogeneous except for some tall equations.
257: The key step is to show that coefficient-wise, the tall equations are implied by the wide equations. We prove this by
258: transforming the problem in terms of tensors of Schur functions and making use of the Hopf algebra structure of symmetric
259: functions; this is achieved in Section~\ref{sec:Hopf}.
260: As we explain in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}, this implies the aforementioned system of linear equations is
261: inconsistent, thus ruling out the existence of a syzygy (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}) and completing the proof.
262: \section{Setting up the linear equations}\label{sec:theequations}
263: We briefly recall the basic properties of Schur functions and Littlewood--Richardson coefficients. See, \emph{e.g.}, \cite{fultonyt, ECII} for thorough introductions. For a partition $\lambda$ (which we identify with its Young diagram in English notation), the \emph{Schur function} $s_\lambda=s_\lambda(x_1,x_2,\ldots)$ is the generating function for semistandard Young tableaux of shape $\lambda$. Schur functions form a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis of $\Lambda$. The \emph{Littlewood--Richardson coefficients} $c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu}$ are the structure constants for the Schur basis:
264: \[s_\lambda s_{\mu} = \sum_{\nu} c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu} s_{\nu}. \]
265: The \emph{Littlewood--Richardson rule} gives an explicit combinatorial description of $c_{\lambda\mu}^{\nu}$
266: (the version we use is Theorem~\ref{thm:pictures}).
267: A fact that we rely on throughout is that Schur functions are homogeneous of degree $|\lambda|=\lambda_1+\lambda_2+\cdots$. In particular, $c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu}$ can only be nonzero if $|\nu|=|\lambda|+|\mu|$. Similarly, it follows from the Littlewood--Richardson rule that $c_{\lambda,\mu}^\nu$ can only be nonzero if $\lambda,\mu\subseteq \nu$ (identifying partitions with their Young diagrams in English notation).
268: For the $\overline{s}_\lambda$ in $\overline{\Lambda}$, the multiplication is the same except partitions $\rho$ must lie in the box $(n-r)^r$ in order for $\overline{s}_\rho$ to be nonzero:
269: \[\overline{s}_\lambda\overline{s}_{\mu} = \sum_{\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r} c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu} \overline{s}_{\nu}. \]
270: Now, we return to addressing Theorem \ref{thm:main}. Fix $v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$, and assign $i,j,a,b$ as in Theorem \ref{thm:main}. To prove the minimality of (\ref{eqn:minimallist}), we must show that for any $\rho\subseteq b^a$, the ring $\overline{\Lambda}$ does not admit syzygies of the form
271: \begin{equation}
272: \label{eqn:goal-syzygy}
273: \overline{s}_{(i^j,\rho)}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\\lambda\neq \rho}}g_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}.
274: \end{equation}
275: There is one important technicality we dispense with immediately: if $i=n-r$, then $a=0$ and Theorem \ref{thm:main} is trivial. Thus we will assume that $i<n-r$, so $a\geq 1$.
276: Actually, we will prove the slightly stronger statement that no expression (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}) exists for any $n,r,i,j,a,b\geq 1$ with
277: \[r<n,\quad a+j\leq r,\quad a+i\leq n-r, \quad \mbox{and}\quad b\leq i,j. \]
278: Fix any such integers $(n,r,i,j,a,b)$. Suppose one can find $\rho\subseteq b^a$ and polynomials $g_{\lambda}\in \overline{\Lambda}$ such that (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}) holds.
279: Since the Schur functions are homogeneous, both $\Lambda$ and $I_{r,n-r}$ are graded by degree. The quotient $\overline{\Lambda}$ inherits this grading. Thus, we can compare the degree $N=|\rho|$ homogeneous components of both sides of (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}) and rearrange to obtain a homogeneous syzygy of the form
280: \begin{equation}
281: \label{eqn:syzygy}
282: \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|=N}}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}f_{\lambda}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)} \quad\mbox{with $f_{\lambda}$ homogeneous and }\deg f_{\lambda} = N- |\lambda|.
283: \end{equation}
284: By homogeneity, the left-hand side of (\ref{eqn:syzygy}) is simply a $\mathbb{Z}$-linear combination (that is, each $f_{\lambda}\in {\mathbb Z}$). We ignore the left-hand side of (\ref{eqn:syzygy}) for now and instead focus on the right-hand side.
285: Since $f_{\lambda}\in \overline{\Lambda}$, we can expand in the Schur basis to obtain
286: \[f_{\lambda} = \sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}A_{\lambda,\theta}\overline{s}_{\theta}. \]
287: Then
288: \begin{align*}
289: \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}
290: &=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}A_{\lambda,\theta}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}\overline{s}_\theta\\
291: &=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}A_{\lambda,\theta}\sum_{\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu}\, \overline{s}_{\nu}\\
292: &=\sum_{\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r}\biggr( \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}\biggr) \overline{s}_{\nu}.
293: \end{align*}
294: Viewing the parameters $A_{\lambda,\theta}$ as indeterminates, a syzygy of the form (\ref{eqn:syzygy}) for some $n,r,i,j,a,b,N$ is equivalent to a solution of the simultaneous linear equations
295: \begin{equation}
296: \label{eqn:eqs}
297: \biggr\{ \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}=\chi(\nu)\,\biggr| \,\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r \biggr\},
298: \end{equation}
299: where $\chi(\nu)=f_\rho$ when $\nu=(i^j,\rho)$ for some $\rho\subseteq b^a$ with $|\rho|=N$, and $\chi(\nu)=0$ otherwise.
300: The following definition records the assumptions on the parameters used in the preceding considerations.
301: \begin{definition}
302: Call an integer tuple $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ \emph{valid} if $n,r,i,j,a,b,N\geq 1$ with
303: \[r<n,\quad a+j\leq r,\quad a+i\leq n-r,\quad b\leq i,j,\quad\mbox{and}\quad N\leq ab. \]
304: \end{definition}
305: Due to vanishing of Littlewood--Richardson coefficients, not all partitions $\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r$ contribute meaningfully to (\ref{eqn:eqs}).
306: \begin{definition}
307: Call a partition $\nu$ with $i^j\subseteq\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r$ and $|\nu|=ij+N$ \emph{allowable}.
308: \end{definition}
309: When $\nu$ is not allowable, both $\chi(\nu)$ and all the Littlewood--Richardson coefficients $c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu}$ vanish. In this case both sides of the corresponding equation in (\ref{eqn:eqs}) are zero, so we can assume that $\nu$ is always allowable in~(\ref{eqn:eqs}). We will actually show that the equations (\ref{eqn:eqs}) are inconsistent by focusing on a smaller subset of them.
310: \begin{definition}
311: Call an allowable partition $\nu$ \emph{decomposable} if $\nu_{j+1}\leq b$ (taking $\nu_{j+1}=0$ if $\ell(\nu)= j$), and $\ell(\nu)\leq j+a$. Write $\decomp_{\varphi}$ for the set of decomposable partitions $\nu$. We will decompose any $\nu\in\decomp_{\varphi}$ into 3 smaller partitions via Figure \ref{fig:decomposition}.
312: \end{definition}
313: We will similarly say that an equation in (\ref{eqn:eqs}) is \emph{decomposable} if the partition $\nu$ it corresponds to is decomposable.
314: \begin{figure}[ht]
315: \begin{center}
316: \includegraphics{Images/nu_decomposition_scaled.pdf}
317: \end{center}
318: \caption{}
319: \label{fig:decomposition}
320: \end{figure}
321: We divide the decomposable partitions into two groups.
322: \begin{definition}
323: We say a decomposable partition $\nu$ is \emph{tall} if $\nu_1=i$ (so $\nu_R$ is empty), and \emph{wide} otherwise.
324: \end{definition}
325: Similarly, refer to the equation of a tall (resp.\ wide) partition $\nu$ as tall (resp.\ wide).
326: \begin{lemma}
327: \label{lem:implication-of-syzygy}
328: Suppose for some valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ there is a homogeneous syzygy of degree $N$ in $\overline{\Lambda}$ of the form
329: \[\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|=N}}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}f_{\lambda}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}\]
330: with the left-hand side nonzero.
331: Then the decomposable equations
332: \begin{equation}
333: \label{eqn:decomposable}
334: \biggr\{ \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}=\chi(\nu)\,\biggr| \,\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi} \biggr\}
335: \end{equation}
336: are consistent, where $\chi(\nu)\in\mathbb{Z}$ is defined for $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$ by
337: \[\chi(\nu)=\begin{cases}
338: f_{\nu_B} &\mbox{ if $\nu$ is tall,}\\
339: 0 &\mbox{ if $\nu$ is wide.}
340: \end{cases} \]
341: \end{lemma}
342: \begin{proof}
343: The above considerations show that the lemma is true if we replace (\ref{eqn:decomposable}) with the superset (\ref{eqn:eqs}). Clearly, removing equations from a consistent linear system yields a consistent linear system.
344: \end{proof}
345: We show that the decomposable equations are inconsistent in Theorem \ref{thm:inconsistent}. We will use the following notation for left-hand sides of the individual equations in (\ref{eqn:decomposable}).
346: \begin{definition}
347: Given a valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ and any $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$, define
348: \[\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)= \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}. \]
349: \end{definition}
350: \begin{example}
351: \label{exp:equations}
352: Let $n=12$, $r=6$, $i=j=a=b=3$, and $N=4$. In this case, the allowable partitions $\nu$ are:
353: \begin{center}
354: \includegraphics[scale=1]{Images/m3n4partitions.pdf}
355: \end{center}
356: These partitions are all decomposable. We record their corresponding linear combinations in Figure~\ref{fig:m3n4equations}.
357: \end{example}
358: \begin{example}
359: If $n=17$, $r=9$, $i=5$, $j=3$, $a=4$, $b=2$, and $N=10$, then not all allowable partitions are decomposable.
360: This is the case for the two partitions shown below.
361: \begin{center}
362: \includegraphics[scale=.8]{Images/indecomposable_partitions.pdf}
363: \end{center}
364: In particular, for these parameter values there are strictly more (nontrivial) equations in (\ref{eqn:eqs}) than in (\ref{eqn:decomposable}). The point of Lemma~\ref{lem:implication-of-syzygy} is that we do not need to use these equations to show the system (\ref{eqn:eqs}) is inconsistent.
365: \end{example}
