373: \section{The Hopf Algebra of symmetric functions}\label{sec:Hopf}
374: Fix a valid tuple $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$. View the linear forms $\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)$ as vectors in the ambient vector space $V$ with basis
375: $\left\{A_{\lambda,\theta} \right\}_{\lambda,\theta}$, where $\lambda,\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r$.
376: Let $T$ be the linear isomorphism $T:V\to \overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ defined by
377: \[T(A_{\lambda,\theta}) = \overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}.\]
378: We use this isomorphism to give a useful perspective on the vectors $\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)\in V$ via
379: the (ring) product structure on $\overline{\Lambda}\otimes\overline{\Lambda}$ and
380: the coproduct map
381: \[\Delta:\Lambda\to \Lambda\otimes\Lambda.\]
382: Recall the coproduct $\Delta$ on symmetric functions acts on Schur functions by
383: \[\Delta(s_{\nu}) = \sum_{\lambda,\mu} c_{\lambda,\mu}^\nu (s_\lambda\otimes s_{\mu}).\]
384: Since the projection $\Lambda\to\overline{\Lambda}$ naturally induces a projection
385: \[\Lambda\otimes \Lambda \to \overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}, \]
386: we will also denote the latter with overlines.
387: \begin{definition}
388: \label{def:cp}
389: We define a linear endomorphism $\mathrm{CP}:\overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}\to \overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ as follows.
390: For a simple tensor $\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_\mu\in \overline{\Lambda}\otimes\overline{\Lambda}$, define
391: \begin{align}
392: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_\mu) = \begin{cases}
393: \overline{\Delta(s_{\lambda})} - (\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes 1) &\mbox{ if $\overline{s}_\mu= 1$},\\
394: \overline{\Delta(s_{\lambda})}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\mu}) &\mbox{ if $\overline{s}_\mu\neq 1$}.
395: \end{cases}
396: \end{align}
397: Define $\mathrm{CP}$ on all of $\overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ by extending linearly.
398: \end{definition}
399: \begin{example}
400: \label{exp:cp-example-calculations}
401: For example with $n=12$ and $r=6$ (as in Example \ref{exp:equations}), one can compute
402: \begin{align*}
403: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{21}\otimes \overline{s}_1) &= \overline{\Delta(s_{21})}(1\otimes \overline{s}_1)\\
404: &=\left(1\otimes \overline{s}_{21}
405: +\overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_{11}
406: +\overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_2
407: +\overline{s}_2\otimes \overline{s}_1
408: + \overline{s}_{11}\otimes \overline{s}_1
409: +\overline{s}_{21}\otimes 1
410: \right)(1\otimes \overline{s}_1)\\
411: &=(1\otimes \overline{s}_{21}\overline{s}_1)
412: +(\overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_{11}\overline{s}_1)
413: +(\overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_2\overline{s}_1)
414: +(\overline{s}_2\otimes \overline{s}_1^2)
415: + (\overline{s}_{11}\otimes \overline{s}_1^2)
416: +(\overline{s}_{21}\otimes \overline{s}_1),
417: \end{align*}
418: and
419: \begin{align*}
420: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{22}\otimes 1) &=\overline{\Delta(s_{22})}- (\overline{s}_{22}\otimes 1)\\
421: &=1\otimes \overline{s}_{22}
422: +\overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_{21}
423: +\overline{s}_2\otimes \overline{s}_2
424: +\overline{s}_{11}\otimes \overline{s}_{11}
425: + \overline{s}_{21}\otimes \overline{s}_1.
426: \end{align*}
427: \end{example}
428: We think of each decomposable $\nu$ as corresponding to the simple tensor $\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R}$. Let us consider how the map $\mathrm{CP}$ acts on this tensor when $\nu$ is tall versus when $\nu$ is wide.
429: When $\nu$ is tall, $|\nu_B|=N$ and $\overline{s}_{\nu_R}=\overline{s}_{(0)}=1$. Then by the first case of Definition \ref{def:cp},
430: \begin{align*}
431: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes 1) &= \overline{\Delta(s_{\nu_B})} - (\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes 1)\\
432: &=\sum_{\lambda\subsetneq \nu_B}\sum_{\theta} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta})\\ &=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq \nu_B\\|\lambda|<N}}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}).
433: \end{align*}
434: When $\nu$ is wide, we have $|\nu_B|<N$ so $|\nu_R|>0$. Then the second case of Definition \ref{def:cp} implies
435: \begin{align*}
436: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R}) &= \overline{\Delta(s_{\nu_B})}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R})\\
437: &=\sum_{\lambda\subseteq \nu_B}\sum_{\mu\subseteq \nu_B} c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu_B} \overline{s}_\lambda \otimes (\overline{s}_\mu \overline{s}_{\nu_R}) \\
438: &=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq \nu_B\\|\lambda|<N}}\sum_{\mu\subseteq \nu_B}\sum_{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu_B} \, c_{\mu,\nu_R}^{\theta} (\overline{s}_{\lambda} \otimes \overline{s}_{\theta})\\
439: &=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq \nu_B\\|\lambda|<N}}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}\left(\sum_{\mu\subseteq \nu_B}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu_B} \, c_{\mu,\nu_R}^{\theta}\right) (\overline{s}_{\lambda} \otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}).
440: \end{align*}
441: Recall $V$ denotes the vector space with basis $\left\{A_{\lambda,\theta} \mid \lambda,\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\right\}$, and $T$ is the linear isomorphism $T:V\to \overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ defined by $T(A_{\lambda,\theta}) = \overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}$.
442: \begin{proposition}
443: \label{prop:eq-to-tensors}
444: For any decomposable partition $\nu$,
445: \[T(\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)) = \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R}).\]
446: \end{proposition}
447: To prove Proposition \ref{prop:eq-to-tensors}, we first review Littlewood--Richardson pictures, a combinatorial model of Littlewood--Richardson coefficients due to James and Peel \cite{James.Peel}, and Zelevinsky \cite{Zelev}.
448: A \emph{skew diagram} is obtained from two partitions $\nu$ and $\lambda$ with
449: $\lambda\subseteq \nu$ by aligning the northwest box of each and taking the set difference. This diagram is
450: denoted $\nu/\lambda$. Any partition $\theta$ is also a skew diagram $\theta=\theta/(0)$.
451: A \emph{picture} between two skew diagrams is a bijection between their boxes such that if a box $A$ is weakly above and weakly left of a box $B$ in either diagram, then the corresponding boxes $A'$ and $B'$ of the other diagram appear in order in the reverse row numbering (the numbering of boxes right-to-left in each row, working top-to-bottom). The following result can be found in \cite[Chapter 5.3]{fultonyt}.
452: \begin{theorem}
453: \label{thm:pictures}
454: The Littlewood--Richardson coefficient $c_{\lambda,\mu}^\nu$ equals the number of pictures between $\nu/\lambda$ and $\mu$.
455: \end{theorem}
456: \begin{example}
457: The Littlewood--Richardson coefficient $c_{3222,431}^{543221}=4$ is witnessed by the pictures in Figure \ref{fig:pictures}.
458: \end{example}
459: \begin{figure}[h]
460: \begin{center}
461: \includegraphics[scale=.75]{Images/pictures.pdf}
462: \end{center}
463: \caption{Example of Theorem~\ref{thm:pictures}.}
464: \label{fig:pictures}
465: \end{figure}
466: \begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:eq-to-tensors}.]
467: We must show that $T(\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)) = \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R})$, that is
468: \[\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R}) = \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq \nu_B\\|\lambda|<N}}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^\nu\, (\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes \overline{s}_\theta). \]
469: Suppose first that $\nu$ is tall. Then as we computed after Example \ref{exp:cp-example-calculations},
470: \[
471: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes 1) =\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq \nu_B\\|\lambda|<N}}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}).
472: \]
473: Thus it suffices to prove that
474: \[ c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^\nu = c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B}\]
475: for all $\lambda,\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r$ with $\lambda\subsetneq \nu_B$ and $|\theta|=N-|\lambda|$. This is immediate from Theorem \ref{thm:pictures}, since the skew shapes $\nu_B/\lambda$ and $\nu/(i^j,\lambda)$ are (essentially) the same.
476: Now, assume that $\nu$ is wide. Again consulting the computations following Example \ref{exp:cp-example-calculations}, we have
477: \[
478: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R})
479: =\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq \nu_B\\|\lambda|<N}}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}\left(\sum_{\mu\subseteq \nu_B}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu_B} \, c_{\mu,\nu_R}^{\theta}\right) (\overline{s}_{\lambda} \otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}).
480: \]
481: Hence it suffices to show that
482: \[c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^\nu=\sum_{\mu\subseteq \nu_B}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu_B} \, c_{\mu,\nu_R}^{\theta}\]
483: for all $\lambda,\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r$ with $\lambda\subseteq \nu_B$, $|\lambda|<N$, and $|\theta|=N-|\lambda|$.
484: Denote the conjugate of a partition $\tau$ by $\tau'$. The map $\omega:\Lambda\to\Lambda$ defined by $\omega(s_\rho)=s_{\rho'}$ for each partition $\rho$ is known to be ring involution (see for instance \cite[Chapter 6.2]{fultonyt}). This implies that $c_{\alpha,\beta}^{\gamma}=c_{\alpha',\beta'}^{\gamma'}$ for any $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$. Thus, the proof is complete if we can show
485: \[c^{\nu'}_{(i^j,\lambda)',\theta'} = \sum_{\mu\subseteq \nu_B} c_{\lambda',\mu'}^{(\nu_B)'}\, c_{\mu',(\nu_R)'}^{\theta'}. \]
486: It straightforward to check that a correspondence of skew diagrams
487: \[\nu'/(i^j,\lambda)' \longleftrightarrow \theta' \]
488: is a picture if and only if it induces pictures
489: \begin{align*}
490: (\nu_B)'/\lambda' &\longleftrightarrow \mu'\subseteq \theta' \mbox{ for some $\mu$, and }\\
491: \theta'/\mu'&\longleftrightarrow (\nu_R)'.\qedhere
492: \end{align*}
493: \end{proof}
494: We aim to show that the linear system (\ref{eqn:decomposable}) of Lemma \ref{lem:implication-of-syzygy} is always inconsistent, yielding a contradiction. The main idea is to show that for each tall $\nu$, one can write $\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\{\linear_{\varphi}(\rho) \mid \rho \mbox{ is wide}\}$. We will see in Lemma \ref{lem:tall-dependent-on-wide} that Algorithm~1 below accomplishes this.
495: \begin{algorithm}
496: \caption{}
497: \label{alg}
498: \begin{algorithmic}
499: \State input valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$.
500: \State input a tall $\nu\in\decomp_{\varphi}$.
501: \\
502: \State initialize the tensor
503: \[\xi^{(0)}\coloneqq \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes 1) =\sum_{\lambda\subsetneq \nu_B}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}). \]
504: \State initialize $m=1$.
505: \While{$\xi^{(m-1)}$ contains a nonzero term of the form
506: \[\gamma(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})) \mbox{ with $|\lambda|>0$,} \mbox{\quad(where $k\geq 1$, and $\gamma\in\mathbb{Z}$)} \]
507: }
508: \State Without expanding the product on the right factor of each tensor, set
509: \begin{align*}
510: \xi^{(m)}&\coloneqq\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))\\
511: &=\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma\overline{\Delta(s_{\lambda})} (1\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))\\
512: &=\xi^{(m-1)}-\sum_{\rho,\tau \subseteq (n-r)^r} \gamma \,c_{\rho,\tau}^{\lambda} (\overline{s}_\rho\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}\overline{s}_\tau )).
513: \end{align*}
514: \State increment $m$.
515: \EndWhile
516: \State \Return $\xi^{(m-1)}$
517: \end{algorithmic}
518: \end{algorithm}
519: We first demonstrate this algorithm, then proceed with analyzing it.
520: \begin{example}
521: \label{exp:coproduct-process}
522: Continuing Example \ref{exp:equations}, $\nu=(3,3,3,3,1)$ is decomposable and tall, with
523: \[ \linear_{\varphi}(\nu) = A_{0,31}+A_{1,3}+A_{1,21}+A_{2,2}+A_{2,11}+A_{3,1}+A_{11,2}+A_{21,1}.\]
524: The corresponding tensor is
525: \begin{align*}
526: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{31}\otimes 1)&=\overline{\Delta(s_{31})}-\overline{s}_{31}\otimes 1\\
527: &=1\otimes \overline{s}_{31} + \overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{3} + \overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{21} + \overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{2} +
528: \overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{11}\\
529: &\phantom{blah}+
530: \overline{s}_{3}\otimes \overline{s}_{1} +
531: \overline{s}_{11}\otimes \overline{s}_{2} +
532: \overline{s}_{21}\otimes \overline{s}_{1}.
533: \end{align*}
534: This tensor is $\xi^{(0)}$. It contains the term $\overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{3}$, so we eliminate it by setting
535: \begin{align*}
536: \xi^{(1)} &= \xi^{(0)} - \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{3}) \\
537: &= \xi^{(0)} - \overline{\Delta(s_{1})}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{3}) \\
538: &= \xi^{(0)} - (1\otimes \overline{s}_{1} + \overline{s}_{1}\otimes 1 )(1\otimes \overline{s}_{3})\\
539: &= \xi^{(0)} - (1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_{3} + \overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{3} )\\
540: &=1\otimes \overline{s}_{31} - 1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_{3} + \overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{21} + \overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{2} +
541: \overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{11}\\
542: &\phantom{blah}+
543: \overline{s}_{3}\otimes \overline{s}_{1} +
544: \overline{s}_{11}\otimes \overline{s}_{2} +
545: \overline{s}_{21}\otimes \overline{s}_{1}.
546: \end{align*}
547: For the second iteration, use the term $\overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{2}$ occurring in $\xi^{(1)}$. We eliminate it with:
548: \begin{align*}
549: \xi^{(2)} &= \xi^{(1)} - \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{2}) \\
550: &= \xi^{(1)} - \overline{\Delta(s_{2})}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{2})
551: \end{align*}
552: \begin{align*}
553: &= \xi^{(1)} - (1\otimes \overline{s}_2 + \overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_1+ \overline{s}_2\otimes 1)(1\otimes \overline{s}_2) \\
554: &= \xi^{(1)} - (1\otimes \overline{s}_2^2 + \overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_1\overline{s}_2+ \overline{s}_2\otimes \overline{s}_2) \\
555: &=1\otimes \overline{s}_{31}
556: - 1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_{3}
557: - 1\otimes \overline{s}_2^2
558: + \overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{21}
559: - \overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_1\overline{s}_2
560: +\overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{11}\\
561: &\phantom{blah}+
562: \overline{s}_{3}\otimes \overline{s}_{1} +
563: \overline{s}_{11}\otimes \overline{s}_{2} +
564: \overline{s}_{21}\otimes \overline{s}_{1}.
565: \end{align*}
566: In the third iteration, we eliminate the term $-\overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_{2}$ occurring in $\xi^{(2)}$. Then
567: \begin{align*}
568: \xi^{(3)} &= \xi^{(2)} + \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_{2}) \\
569: &= \xi^{(2)} + \overline{\Delta(s_{1})}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_{2}) \\
570: &= \xi^{(2)} + (1\otimes \overline{s}_1 + \overline{s}_1\otimes 1)(1\otimes \overline{s}_1\overline{s}_2) \\
571: &= \xi^{(2)} + (1\otimes \overline{s}_1^2\overline{s}_2 + \overline{s}_1\otimes \overline{s}_1\overline{s}_2)\\
572: &=1\otimes \overline{s}_{31}
573: - 1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_{3}
574: - 1\otimes \overline{s}_2^2
575: + 1\otimes \overline{s}_1^2\overline{s}_2
576: + \overline{s}_{1}\otimes \overline{s}_{21}
577: +\overline{s}_{2}\otimes \overline{s}_{11}\\
578: &\phantom{blah}+
579: \overline{s}_{3}\otimes \overline{s}_{1} +
580: \overline{s}_{11}\otimes \overline{s}_{2} +
581: \overline{s}_{21}\otimes \overline{s}_{1}.
582: \end{align*}
583: Continuing in this fashion, one eventually arrives at
584: \begin{align*}
585: &1\otimes \overline{s}_{31}
586: - 2(1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_3)
587: - 2(1\otimes \overline{s}_1\overline{s}_{21})
588: - (1\otimes \overline{s}_2^2)
589: -2(1\otimes \overline{s}_{2}\overline{s}_{11})\\
590: &\phantom{blah}
591: +6(1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}^2\overline{s}_2)
592: +3(1\otimes \overline{s}_1^2\overline{s}_{11})
593: -3(1\otimes \overline{s}_1^4)
594: \end{align*}
595: at which point the algorithm terminates.
596: \end{example}
597: Now, we analyze this algorithm in general.
598: \begin{proposition}
599: \label{clm:1}
600: Algorithm~1 terminates after a finite number of steps.
601: \end{proposition}
602: \begin{proof}
603: To prove this claim, consider an iteration
604: \begin{align*}
605: \xi^{(m)}&=\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))\\
606: &=\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma \sum_{\rho,\tau \subseteq (n-r)^r} c_{\rho,\tau}^{\lambda} (\overline{s}_\rho\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}\overline{s}_\tau )).
607: \end{align*}
608: All tensors appearing with nonzero coefficient in the sum have left tensor factor $\overline{s}_{\rho}$ with $|\rho|<|\lambda|$ except for $\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})$, which cancels with the identical term in $\xi^{(m-1)}$. Therefore the number of tensors $s_{\alpha}\otimes \bullet$ appearing in $\xi^{(m)}$ with $|\alpha|\geq |\lambda|$
609: is strictly smaller than the number in $\xi^{(m-1)}$.
610: Hence for $i$ sufficiently large, the number of tensors $s_{\alpha}\otimes \bullet$ appearing in $\xi^{(m)}$ with $|\alpha|>1$ will be zero, at which point the algorithm terminates.
611: \end{proof}
612: \begin{proposition}
613: \label{clm:2}
614: The terms
615: \[\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})\]
616: occurring with nonzero coefficient in any $\xi^{(m)}$ have integral coefficients with sign $(-1)^{k+1}$.
617: \end{proposition}
618: \begin{proof}
619: That the coefficients are integral holds since they arise from repeated sums, differences, and products of Littlewood--Richardson coefficients, which are integers. By the construction of Algorithm 1, the signs start positive in $\xi^{(0)}$ and change each time a new factor is added to the right of the tensor in an update $\xi^{(m-1)}\to \xi^{(m)}$.
620: \end{proof}
621: \begin{proposition}
622: \label{clm:3}
623: The output of Algorithm~1 does not depend on the choice of term in each iteration.
624: \end{proposition}
625: \begin{proof}It follows from Proposition~\ref{clm:2} that during the algorithm, the only cancellation that occurs in an update
626: \begin{equation}
627: \label{eqn:theupdate}
628: \xi^{(m)}=\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))
629: \end{equation}
630: is the cancellation of the term $\gamma\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})$ in $\xi^{(m-1)}$, and its negative in $\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))$. Moreover, the algorithm only can
631: eliminate a term $\gamma\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})$ with $|\lambda|>1$ by the update step
632: (\ref{eqn:theupdate}). Therefore the order in which we compute the update steps (\ref{eqn:theupdate}) does not effect the output.\end{proof}
633: \begin{definition}
634: Given a valid tuple $\varphi$ and a tall partition $\nu\in\decomp_{\varphi}$, let $\mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu)\in\overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ denote the output of Algorithm~1 .
635: \end{definition}
636: \begin{example}
637: Continuing Example \ref{exp:coproduct-process} with $\nu=(3,3,3,3,1)$,
638: \begin{align*}
639: \mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu)&=
640: 1\otimes \overline{s}_{31}
641: - 2(1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_3)
642: - 2(1\otimes \overline{s}_1\overline{s}_{21})
643: - (1\otimes \overline{s}_2^2)
644: -2(1\otimes \overline{s}_{2}\overline{s}_{11})\\
645: &\phantom{blah}+6(1\otimes \overline{s}_{1}^2\overline{s}_2)
646: +3(1\otimes \overline{s}_1^2\overline{s}_{11})
647: -3(1\otimes \overline{s}_1^4).
648: \end{align*}
649: Simplifying, we obtain
650: \begin{align*}
651: \mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu) &= 1\otimes
652: \left(
653: \overline{s}_{31}
654: - 2 \overline{s}_{1}\overline{s}_3
655: - 2 \overline{s}_1\overline{s}_{21}
656: - \overline{s}_2^2
657: -2 \overline{s}_{2}\overline{s}_{11}
658: +6 \overline{s}_{1}^2\overline{s}_2
659: +3\overline{s}_1^2\overline{s}_{11}
660: -3\overline{s}_1^4
661: \right)\\
662: &=-(1\otimes \overline{s}_{211}).
663: \end{align*}
664: Notice $211$ is the conjugate of $\nu_B=31$.
665: \end{example}
666: Denote the conjugate of a partition $\tau$ by $\tau'$. In general, we have:
667: \begin{proposition}
668: \label{prop:hopf-identity-application}
669: Let $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$ be tall with $\nu_B\neq \emptyset$.
670: Then
671: \[\mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu) =
672: (-1)^{|\nu_B|+1}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{(\nu_B)'}). \]
673: \end{proposition}
674: \begin{proof}
675: We proceed by induction on $N=|\nu_B|$. The base case is $|\nu_B|=1$. In this case, Algorithm~1 outputs $\xi^{(0)}=\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_1\otimes 1)$, so
676: \[\mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu)=\xi^{(0)}=\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_1\otimes 1) = \sum_{|\theta|=1} c_{(0),\theta}^{(1)} (1\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}) = 1\otimes \overline{s}_1.\]
677: Suppose $|\nu_B|=k>1$. Construct the tensor $\xi^{(0)}$ from $\nu$, so
678: \begin{align*}
679: \xi^{(0)}&=\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes 1)\\
680: &=\sum_{\lambda\subsetneq \nu_B}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}).
681: \end{align*}
682: Since there are finitely many nonzero terms occurring in the double sum, let us list them out explicitly as
683: \[
684: \xi^{(0)}= (1\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_B})+\sum_{p=1}^L c_{\lambda^p,\theta^p}^{\nu_B}(\overline{s}_{\lambda^p}\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta^p}),
685: \]
686: where $1\leq |\lambda^p|<k$ for each $p$. Set $\varphi^{p} = (n,r,i,j,a,b,|\lambda^p|)$ for each $p$.
687: From Proposition \ref{clm:3}, it follows that $\mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}$ satisfies the ``depth-first search'' recurrence
688: \[\mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu) = (1\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_B}) - \sum_{p=1}^L c_{\lambda^p,\theta^p}^{\nu_B} \mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(i^j,\lambda^p)(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta^p}).\]
689: By the induction assumption,
690: \[\mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi^{p}}(i^j,\lambda^p)(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta^p}) = (-1)^{|\lambda^p|+1}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{(\lambda^p)'}\overline{s}_{\theta^p}) \text{ for each }1\leq p\leq L. \]
691: Thus,
692: \begin{align*}
693: \mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu) &= (1\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_B}) + \sum_{p=1}^L c_{\lambda^p,\theta^p}^{\nu_B} (-1)^{|\lambda^p|}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{(\lambda^p)'}\overline{s}_{\theta^p})\\
694: &=(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_B}) +\sum_{\substack{|\lambda|\geq 1\\ \lambda\subsetneq \nu_B}}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (-1)^{|\lambda|}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\lambda'}\overline{s}_{\theta})\\
695: &=\sum_{\lambda\subsetneq \nu_B}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (-1)^{|\lambda|}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\lambda'}\overline{s}_{\theta}).
696: \end{align*}
697: The lemma then follows if one can prove that
698: \[\sum_{\lambda,\theta}(-1)^{|\lambda|}c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B}\overline{s}_{\lambda'}\overline{s}_{\theta}=0.\]
699: In Lemma \ref{lem:hopf-identity} below, we prove an analogous identity holds in $\Lambda$.
700: Projecting from $\Lambda$ down to $\overline{\Lambda}$ via the (linear) quotient map $s_\lambda\mapsto \overline{s}_{\lambda}$ completes the proof.
701: \end{proof}
702: \begin{lemma}
703: \label{lem:hopf-identity}
704: For any partitions $\lambda,\mu$ and any partition $\nu\neq \emptyset$,
705: \[\sum_{\lambda,\mu}(-1)^{|\lambda|}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu}s_{\lambda'}s_{\mu}=0.\]
706: \end{lemma}
707: \begin{proof}
708: Recall the Hopf algebra structure on $\Lambda$ over $\mathbb{Z}$. This includes
709: \begin{itemize}
710: \item the usual multiplication map $\nabla:\Lambda\otimes \Lambda\to \Lambda$ with
711: \[\nabla(s_{\lambda}\otimes s_{\mu}) = s_{\lambda}s_{\mu};\]
712: \item the coproduct described previously, with $\Delta:\Lambda\to\Lambda\otimes\Lambda$ with
713: \[\Delta(s_\nu) = \sum_{\lambda,\mu}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu}s_{\lambda}\otimes s_{\nu}; \]
714: \item the unit $\eta:\mathbb{Z}\to \Lambda$, the ring homomorphism with $1\mapsto 1$;
715: \item the counit $\epsilon:\Lambda\to\mathbb{Z}$ taking $f\in \lambda$ to its constant term $f(0,0,\ldots)$;
716: \item the antipode $S:\Lambda\to\Lambda$ with
717: \[S(s_{\lambda})=(-1)^{|\lambda|}s_{\lambda'}.\]
718: \end{itemize}
719: The antipode $S$ is characterized by the commutative diagram shown in Figure \ref{fig:diag}.
720: \begin{figure}[ht]
721: \[
722: \begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3.6em,column sep=1em]
723: & \Lambda\otimes \Lambda \arrow[rr,"S\otimes\mathrm{id}"] && \Lambda\otimes \Lambda \arrow[dr,"\nabla"] \\
724: \Lambda \arrow[ur,"\Delta"] \arrow[rr,"\varepsilon"] \arrow[dr,"\Delta"'] && \mathbb{Z} \arrow[rr,"\eta"] && \Lambda \\
725: & \Lambda\otimes \Lambda \arrow[rr,"\mathrm{id}\otimes S"'] && \Lambda\otimes \Lambda \arrow[ur,"\nabla"']
726: \end{tikzcd}
727: \]
728: \caption{The Hopf algebra stucture on $\Lambda$}
729: \label{fig:diag}
730: \end{figure}
731: On a Schur function $s_{\nu}$, we compute
732: \begin{align*}
733: \eta\circ\epsilon(s_{\nu}) &= \eta(0) = 0,\quad\mbox{and}\\
734: \nabla\circ(S\otimes \mathrm{id})\circ\Delta(s_{\nu})&=\nabla\left( \sum_{\lambda,\mu}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu}S(s_{\lambda})\otimes s_{\nu}\right) = \sum_{\lambda,\mu}c_{\lambda,\mu}^{\nu} (-1)^{|\lambda|}s_{\lambda'}s_\mu. \qedhere
735: \end{align*}
736: \end{proof}
737: \section{Conclusion of the proof} \label{sec:conclusion}
738: \begin{definition}
739: Let $W$ be the subspace
740: \[W= \mathrm{Span}_{\mathbb{Z}}\left\{\linear_{\varphi}(\rho)\mid \rho\in\decomp_{\varphi} \text{ is wide} \right\}\]
741: \end{definition}
742: Recall the linear isomorphism $T:V\to \overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ defined by $T(A_{\lambda,\theta}) = \overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}$.
743: \begin{lemma}
744: \label{lem:tall-dependent-on-wide}
745: Let $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ be valid with $N\leq \min(j,n-r-i)$. Then
746: $\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)\in W$
747: for any tall $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$.
748: \end{lemma}
749: \begin{proof}
750: It is enough to show the analogous statement for tensors:
751: \[\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R})\in \mathrm{Span}_{\mathbb{Z}}\left\{\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\rho_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\rho_R})\mid \rho\in\decomp_{\varphi} \text{ is wide} \right\} = T(W).\]
752: From $\varphi$ and $\nu$, suppose Algorithm~1 produced $\xi^{(0)},\ldots,\xi^{(K)}= \mathrm{Reduce}_{\varphi}(\nu)$. Then
753: \begin{align*}
754: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\nu_R})&=\xi^{(0)}\\
755: &=\xi^{(K)} -\sum_{m=1}^K (\xi^{(m)}-\xi^{(m-1)})
756: \end{align*}
757: By Proposition \ref{prop:hopf-identity-application},
758: \[\xi^{(K)}= (-1)^{|\nu_B|+1}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{(\nu_B)'}).\]
759: We have $\nu_B'\subseteq (b^a)'=a^b$. Since $a+i\leq n-r$ and $b\leq j$, it follows that $\xi^{(K)}\in T(W)$.
760: It remains to show that each term $(\xi^{(m)}-\xi^{(m-1)})\in T(W)$. By definition,
761: \[\xi^{(m)}-\xi^{(m-1)} = -\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})) \]
762: for some $\gamma\in \mathbb{Z}$ and partitions $\lambda,\mu^{(1)},\ldots,\mu^{(k)}$.
763: One observes from Algorithm~1 that \[|\lambda|+|\mu^{(1)}|+\cdots+|\mu^{(k)}|=N.\]
764: Since we are assuming $N\leq \min(j,n-r-i)$,
765: \[ \prod_{p=1}^k \overline{s}_{\mu^{(p)}}\in \mathrm{Span}_\mathbb{Z}\left\{\overline{s}_\theta\mid \theta\subseteq (n-r-i)^j\right\},\]
766: say
767: \[\prod_{p=1}^k \overline{s}_{\mu^{(p)}} = \sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r-i)^j \\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} d_{\theta}\overline{s}_{\theta}. \]
768: Then if we expand, we obtain
769: \begin{align*}
770: \xi^{(m)}-\xi^{(m-1)}
771: &=-\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))\\
772: &=-\gamma\mathrm{CP}\biggr(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes \sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r-i)^j \\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} d_{\theta}\overline{s}_{\theta} \biggr)\\
773: &=-\gamma \sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r-i)^j \\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} d_{\theta}\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}).
774: \end{align*}
775: Since $\lambda\subseteq \nu_B \subseteq b^a)$, each term $\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta})$ is of the form $\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\rho_B}\otimes \overline{s}_{\rho_R})$ with $\rho\in\decomp_{\varphi}$ wide, it follows that $\xi^{(m)}-\xi^{(m-1)} \in T(W)$.
776: \end{proof}
777: \begin{lemma}
778: \label{lem:restricting-equations}
779: Suppose $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ be valid. Set $\widehat{\varphi}=(\widehat{n},\widehat{r},i,\widehat{j},a,b,N)$ where $\widehat{n}=4^qn$, $\widehat{r}=2^q r$, and $\widehat{j}=2^q j$ for $q\in {\mathbb N}$. Fix any $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$, and let $\widehat{\nu}\in\decomp_{\widehat{\varphi}}$ be the partition with
780: \[\widehat{\nu}_B=\nu_B \quad\mbox{and}\quad \widehat{\nu}_R=\nu_R.\]
781: Then setting the variables
782: \[\{A_{\lambda,\theta} \mid \lambda\nsubseteq b^a \mbox{ or } \theta\nsubseteq (n-r-i)^j \}\]
783: to zero in $\linear_{\widehat{\varphi}}(\widehat{\nu})$ yields exactly $\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)$.
784: \end{lemma}
785: \begin{proof}
786: It suffices to note that
787: \[c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu}= c_{(i^{\widehat{j}}\lambda),\theta}^{\widehat{\nu}}\]
788: by Theorem \ref{thm:pictures}, since the skew shapes $\nu/(i^j,\lambda)$ and $\widehat{\nu}/(i^{\widehat{j}}\lambda)$ are (essentially) the same.
789: \end{proof}
790: \begin{theorem}
791: \label{thm:inconsistent}
792: For any valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$, the equations
793: \begin{equation}
794: \label{eqn:all-equations}
795: \left\{ \linear_{\varphi}(\nu) = d_{\nu}\mid \nu\in \decomp_{\varphi} \right\}
796: \end{equation}
797: are inconsistent whenever $d_{\nu}=0$ for all wide $\nu$, and $d_{\rho}\neq 0$ for some tall $\rho$.
798: \end{theorem}
799: \begin{proof}
800: Suppose first that
801: \[N\leq \min(j,n-r-i).\] By Lemma \ref{lem:tall-dependent-on-wide}, we have $\linear_{\varphi}(\rho) \in W$.
802: The restriction $d_\nu=0$ when $\nu$ is wide would then force $d_{\rho}=0$, contradicting the hypothesis. Thus the equations (\ref{eqn:all-equations}) are inconsistent.
803: Now suppose that
804: \[N>\min(j,n-r-i).\]
805: Choose $q$ so that $N\leq \min(2^qj,4^qn-2^qr-i)$. Let
806: \[\widehat{\varphi} = (\widehat{n},\widehat{r},i,\widehat{j},a,b,N),\quad \mbox{where} \quad \widehat{n}=4^qn,\quad \widehat{r}=2^qr,\quad\mbox{and}\quad \widehat{j}=2^qj.\]
807: For each $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$, let $\widehat{\nu}\in\decomp_{\widehat{\varphi}}$ be the partition with $\widehat{\nu}_B=\nu_B$ and $\widehat{\nu}_R=\nu_R$. For $\tau\in\decomp_{\widehat{\varphi}}$, define
808: \[e_{\tau} = \begin{cases}
809: d_\nu &\mbox{ if } \tau=\widehat{\nu} \mbox{ for some } \nu\in\decomp_{\varphi},\\
810: 0 &\mbox{ otherwise.}
811: \end{cases} \]
812: By the previous argument, the equations
813: \begin{equation}
814: \label{eqn:bigger_m}
815: \left\{ \linear_{\widehat{\varphi}}(\tau) = e_{\tau}\mid \tau\in \decomp_{\widehat{\varphi}} \right\}
816: \end{equation}
817: are inconsistent. By Lemma \ref{lem:restricting-equations}, the equations (\ref{eqn:all-equations}) can be obtained by setting certain variables to zero in (\ref{eqn:bigger_m}). Setting variables to zero in an inconsistent linear system produces the same.
818: \end{proof}
819: \begin{theorem}
820: For any valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$, there is no syzygy of the form
821: \[\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|=N}}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}f_{\lambda}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}\]
822: with the left-hand side nonzero.
823: \end{theorem}
824: \begin{proof}
825: By Lemma \ref{lem:implication-of-syzygy}, the existence of such a syzygy would contradict Theorem \ref{thm:inconsistent}.
826: \end{proof}
827: \begin{corollary}
828: \label{cor:no-syzygy}
829: For any bigrassmannian $v=v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$ and $i,j,a,b$ as in Theorem \ref{thm:main}, the generating set
830: \[\{\overline{s}_{\mu} \mid i^j\subseteq \mu \subseteq (i^j,b^a)\}\]
831: of $J_v$ in $\overline{\Lambda}$ is minimal.
832: \end{corollary}
833: \begin{remark}
834: In \cite{RWY}, a transposed version of Theorem \ref{thm:main} is also proved (with minimality conjectured). The generating set is
835: \begin{equation}
836: \label{eqn:transposedlist}
837: \{\overline{s}_{\mu} \mid i^j\subseteq \mu \subseteq ((i+a)^b,i^{j-b})\}.
838: \end{equation}
839: These are exactly the conjugates of the generating set (\ref{eqn:minimallist}) whose minimality we prove. One could straightforwardly``transpose'' all arguments in this paper to prove minimality of (\ref{eqn:transposedlist}). Specifically, one would interchange the roles of wide and
840: tall equations in Section~\ref{sec:theequations} (with minor modifications) and swap the order of the tensor components in Sections~\ref{sec:Hopf} and \ref{sec:conclusion}.
841: \end{remark}
